@Commissar_tomcat Depends on how you set up the automation. It could just as easily be set up to work around everyone's projected leave on the days that people are most likely to take leave, and account for people actually booking leave, and adjust over time. (And notify people who aren't taking leave, or just automatically schedule it for them.) 
Hell, given that a computer can keep track of details easily too, it wouldn't be hard for a shift scheduling AI to work things out with respect to distance people have to travel, method of travel and its likelihood of delays, commitments already noted, staffing needs, busyness, and all the millionty other factors, and eventually you'd have schedules that respect everyone's time but also keep the place staffed. 
It's a lot easier for a computer to collate all that data and figure out a fair schedule (or where the problem is that's causing staffing issues) than it is for a human to do that. And the computer is less likely to forget details like the nuances of labour laws, or who has school pickup commitments.

