@Magic-gt4pl I'm afraid, I do not really agree. We've seen what has happened with price increases in today's market. Prices have risen, far outpacing inflationary causes or the cost of goods. A large portion is driven by maximization of profit, so I do think that if everyone's income just increases the prices of everyday goods will just rise in-tandem.

I think another analogy would be prices in different markets. The prices of food in the USA is significantly more expensive than, in say, Southeast Asia. A large portion of that is the cost to produce those goods is lower in those areas, but the prices do follow the willigness-to-pay of the local economy.

Lastly, a surplus of money injected into the economy is almost always inflationary.

